---
/**
 * /solvers/precision/: precision as a type parameter.
 *
 * Sources: examples/avionics_examples/cfd/turbulence_flow/output.txt (forecast horizons, machine
 * epsilon, the horizon law), deep_causality_cfd/verification/mms_taylor_green_verification
 * (README for the f32 and Float106 errors, baseline.txt for f64), the corridor README (the
 * 106-bit and f32 records) and src/data/boundaries.ts (precision-alias-drift).
 *
 * The corridor's 106-bit result and its 11x ratio come from an earlier build and have no committed
 * timing artifact; the page states both facts next to the ratio.
 */
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Precision as a parameter | DeepCausality CFD';
const description =
  'Change one type alias and rerun the same program at f32, f64 or Float106. The Taylor–Green kernel error and the forecast horizon of a chaotic flow at each precision.';

const alias = `/// Working precision.
pub type FloatType = f64; // or f32, or deep_causality_num::Float106`;

const lift = `use deep_causality_num::lift;

// A specification constant is an exact f64 literal, lifted into the working precision.
let q_max = lift::<FloatType>(60.0);`;

const rerun = `# In deep_causality_cfd/verification/mms_taylor_green_verification/main.rs, change the alias
# (for Float106, also add \`use deep_causality_num::Float106;\`), then run:
cargo run --release -p deep_causality_cfd --example mms_taylor_green_verification`;

/** One row per precision: machine epsilon and the horizon come from the turbulence example, the kernel error from the Taylor-Green target. */
const ladder = [
  { alias: 'f32', eps: '1.2e-7', err: '3e-8', horizon: '21.5' },
  { alias: 'f64', eps: '2.2e-16', err: '1e-16', horizon: '44.5' },
  { alias: 'Float106', eps: '1.0e-32', err: '8e-33', horizon: 'about 81' },
];
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/solvers/precision/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Solvers · Precision</p>
    <h1>Precision as a parameter</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Change one type alias and the same program reruns at <code>f32</code>, <code>f64</code> or
      <code>Float106</code>, a 106-bit type. In the turbulence example the forecast horizon of a
      chaotic flow grows from 21.5 to 44.5 to about 81 time units across the three.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="measured">
    <h2>What do the three precisions measure?</h2>
    <p>
      Two committed programs run the same code at all three precisions. The Taylor–Green check
      measures the error of the incompressible flow’s right-hand-side kernel against the exact
      rate of change. The turbulence example forecasts a chaotic flow and reports the forecast
      horizon: the lead time before the flow state is off by one state-space unit.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Alias</th>
            <th scope="col">Machine epsilon</th>
            <th scope="col">Taylor–Green kernel error</th>
            <th scope="col">Forecast horizon</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {ladder.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>{r.alias}</code></th>
              <td class="num" data-label="Machine epsilon">{r.eps}</td>
              <td class="num" data-label="Taylor–Green kernel error">{r.err}</td>
              <td class="num" data-label="Forecast horizon">{r.horizon}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Each 16 extra digits roughly double the trustworthy forecast time. The run measures the
      <code>f32</code> and <code>f64</code> horizons. At <code>Float106</code> the horizon lies
      beyond the T = 60 the run covers, so the figure of about 81 comes from the law ln(1/ε)/λ with
      λ ≈ 0.906, which gives 81.3. The same law gives 17.6 and 39.8 for the other two, below the
      measured 21.5 and 44.5, so read 81 as an estimate.
    </p>
    <p>
      The forecast is the Lorenz system, a three-variable truncation of Rayleigh–Bénard
      convection, and not a Navier–Stokes solution. It shows how precision limits the lead time of
      any chaotic forecast, and it runs the same <code>Rk4</code> march at each precision. The
      <a href="/examples/turbulence-flow/">turbulence example</a> holds the full run. The kernel
      errors come from the <a href="/validation/#mms-tgv">Taylor–Green record</a>. Its committed
      baseline holds the <code>f64</code> run; the <code>f32</code> and <code>Float106</code>
      figures come from the target’s README.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="alias">
    <h2>One alias sets the precision of the whole run</h2>
    <p>
      Every theory, solver, stage and observable is generic over a single real scalar. A program
      fixes the alias and the whole run follows it. The program converts to <code>f64</code> only
      at the display boundary.
    </p>
    <Code code={alias} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>
      Specification constants stay exact <code>f64</code> literals and enter the computation
      through <code>lift</code>. Changing the alias then reruns the same program at another
      precision, and does not round its inputs first.
    </p>
    <Code code={lift} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>To rerun the Taylor–Green check at another precision:</p>
    <Code code={rerun} lang="bash" themes={SHIKI} />
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="corridor">
    <h2>The plasma-blackout corridor does not currently compile at 106-bit</h2>
    <div class="callout callout-warn">
      <p class="callout-title">Limit</p>
      <p>
        Switching the corridor’s alias to <code>Float106</code> fails to compile. The generic
        scalar reaches most of the way, but the corridor example has accumulated
        <code>f64</code>-specific code, and a handful of spots in the crate have too. The
        <a href="/boundaries/#precision-alias-drift">limits page</a> lists the compile errors.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      An earlier, superseded build of the corridor did run at 106-bit. Every gate and every
      discrete event step came out identical to the <code>f64</code> run, the continuous
      witnesses agreed to 15 or 16 significant digits, and the run cost roughly eleven times the
      wall clock. That record has no committed timing artifact, and its <code>f64</code> baseline
      of about 35 s disagrees with the 44.4 s the committed output records today. Treat the 11×
      ratio and the identical-gates result as history, not as a result you can reproduce. Take
      the working ladder from the table above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Precision limits the bottom of the range as well as the top. On that earlier build the
      corridor at <code>f32</code> crashed at step 1: <code>h²</code> in its Saha kernel (4.4e-67)
      underflows the <code>f32</code> exponent range. The position ulp at Earth radius is about
      0.5 m, which would swallow a sub-meter navigation result anyway.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/solvers/">The three solvers</a>
      <a href="/validation/#mms-tgv">The Taylor–Green record</a>
      <a href="/why/#algorithms">Why error attribution</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-weight: 400;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; }
  tbody td { color: var(--fg-1); }
  tbody tr:last-child th, tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
  .doc-sec :global(pre.astro-code) { max-width: none; }
</style>
